const LECTURAS = {
 "fil-lec-platon-aristoteles": {
  "subject": "fil",
  "tema": "Philosophy · Topic 1 (what is philosophy?)",
  "title": "Plato and Aristotle: why do we start to philosophise?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 1 (what is philosophy?). Two classic answers to why we start to philosophise and what it is for.</p>\n<h2>Setting the scene</h2>\n<p>In 399 BC a court in Athens tried Socrates, accused of not respecting the gods of the city and of corrupting the young. He was sentenced to drink hemlock. Socrates wrote nothing; we know him mainly through his disciple <strong>Plato</strong> (427–347 BC), who a few years later wrote the <em>Apology</em>, the defence speech of his teacher as he chose to remember it. How much of it is Socrates and how much Plato is still debated.</p>\n<p><strong>Aristotle</strong> (384–322 BC) arrived at Plato’s Academy at the age of seventeen and stayed there for twenty years. At the beginning of the <em>Metaphysics</em> he tries to clarify what kind of knowledge he is seeking, and to do so he asks how philosophy began. Both texts therefore answer the question of this topic.</p>\n\n<h2>1. An oracle that cannot be understood</h2>\n<blockquote>Chaerephon was a companion of mine from our youth and a friend of your party; he shared exile with you and returned with you. You know what he was like: vehement in everything he undertook. Well, he once went to Delphi and dared to ask this —and do not make an uproar, Athenians—: he asked whether there was anyone wiser than I. And the Pythia replied that no one was wiser. […] When I heard it, I kept turning it over in my mind: ‘What can the god mean? What riddle is this? For I know that I am not wise, either much or little. What does he mean, then, by saying that I am the wisest? Surely he is not lying: it is not permitted to him.’</blockquote>\n<p>Socrates stands before a jury that can sentence him to death, and before defending himself against the charges he wants to explain where his reputation for wisdom comes from. The story begins with a problem: the oracle of Delphi, which speaks in the name of the god Apollo, says that no one is wiser than he is, and Socrates is convinced that he knows nothing important. The two things cannot both be simply true. Instead of accepting the praise or rejecting it, Socrates does something new: he treats the god’s statement as a riddle to be investigated. That is where his philosophy begins.</p>\n\n<h2>2. The politicians: believing that one knows</h2>\n<blockquote>I went to see one of those who pass for wise, thinking that there, if anywhere, I would refute the prophecy […]. I examined him thoroughly —there is no need to give his name; he was one of the politicians— and this is what happened to me: it seemed to me that this man appeared wise to many people and above all to himself, and that he was not. […] I went away thinking to myself: I am wiser than this man. It is probable that neither of us knows anything good or beautiful; but he thinks he knows something without knowing it, and I, since I do not know it, do not think I know it either. It seems that I have this small advantage over him: that what I do not know, I do not think I know.</blockquote>\n<p>This is the core of the text. Socrates does not beat the politician because he knows more things, but because he knows where his own knowledge ends. ‘Anything good or beautiful’ refers to what matters most to a Greek: how one ought to live. Neither of them knows it, but the politician believes he does and Socrates does not. That ‘small advantage’ is what is usually summed up as ‘I only know that I know nothing’, a sentence Socrates does not say in that form. It is not a confession of total ignorance, but a form of honesty: not confusing what one believes with what one truly knows.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Poets and craftsmen: knowing how to do is not knowing</h2>\n<blockquote>I then understood that poets do not do what they do out of wisdom, but by a kind of natural gift and in a trance, like seers and those who chant oracles: they too say many beautiful things, but they know nothing of what they say. Finally I went to the craftsmen. […] they knew what I did not know, and in that they were wiser than I. But it seemed to me that the good craftsmen had the same defect as the poets: because they performed their trade well, each of them also thought himself the wisest in the greatest matters, and that discord hid the wisdom they did have.</blockquote>\n<p>The Greek word <em>sophia</em> was used for the wise person and also for the skilled professional, which is why Socrates looks for wisdom in three places: power, poetry and the workshop. Poets write beautiful things, but when asked what they mean they cannot explain it: they have inspiration, not knowledge. Craftsmen do truly know something, their trade. Their mistake is a different one: assuming that, because they are good at one thing, they are also experts on the big questions. Socrates calls it a ‘discord’, like a false note that spoils the whole melody. Think of someone who is a good footballer or a good doctor and gives opinions with the same confidence about everything else.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Human wisdom is worth little</h2>\n<blockquote>From this examination, Athenians, many enmities have arisen against me, and of the harshest and most burdensome kind […]. The fact is, gentlemen, that probably the truly wise one is the god, and that in this oracle he is saying that human wisdom is worth little, or nothing. And it seems that he is not speaking of this Socrates, but is using my name and taking me as an example, as if to say: ‘The wisest among you, humans, is the one who, like Socrates, has realised that in truth he is worth nothing with respect to wisdom.’</blockquote>\n<p>In the end the riddle is solved: the oracle was not praising Socrates, it was using him as an example. Complete wisdom belongs to the gods; the best a human being can do is to recognise their limits. But this conclusion has a price. Examining others and showing them that they do not know what they think they know makes people look ridiculous in front of others, and that creates enemies. Thus Socrates’s philosophy appears as an uncomfortable and risky activity, which will end up costing him his life.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Aristotle: everything begins with wonder</h2>\n<blockquote>For it is owing to their wonder that men both now begin and at first began to philosophise: at first they wondered at the oddities close at hand, and then, advancing little by little, they raised questions about greater matters, such as the changes of the moon, what happens with the sun and the stars, and the origin of the universe. And whoever doubts and wonders recognises that he does not know; that is why the lover of myths is in a way a philosopher too, because myth is made of wonderful things.</blockquote>\n<p>Aristotle is not on trial: he writes calmly about the origin of knowledge. His answer is wonder (in Greek, <em>thaumazein</em>), which does not mean admiration or enthusiasm, but being struck by something that does not fit and ceasing to take it for granted. First small, nearby things puzzle us; then, great ones: the stars, the origin of everything. It is striking that he also recognises something of the philosopher in whoever loves myths, because myth is born from the same puzzlement. The difference is that the philosopher does not settle for a story: he looks for the causes.</p>\n\n<h2>6. The only free science</h2>\n<blockquote>So if they philosophised to escape from ignorance, it is clear that they pursued knowledge for the sake of knowing and not for any utility. What actually happened bears witness to this: this kind of knowledge began to be sought when almost everything necessary was already provided, and also what makes life comfortable and pleasant. […] just as we call a man free who exists for himself and not for another, so we call this the only free science among the sciences, because it alone exists for itself.</blockquote>\n<p>Aristotle gives two proofs that philosophy is not sought for its usefulness. The first looks at the <strong>why</strong>: if one philosophises to escape ignorance, what is wanted is to know, not to profit. The second looks at the <strong>when</strong>: people only began to philosophise when basic needs were met and there was free time. That is why he compares it to the free man of his day, who lived for himself and not in the service of another (remember that in Greece there were slaves). A ‘free’ kind of knowledge is one that is worth having for its own sake, not as a tool for something else.</p>\n\n<h2>7. From wonder to knowledge: the diagonal</h2>\n<blockquote>Everyone begins by wondering that things are as they are, just as before fairground automata, or the solstices, or the fact that the diagonal cannot be measured by the side […]. But we must end in the opposite state and, as the proverb says, in the better one, as happens in these cases when one learns: nothing would astonish a geometer so much as the diagonal turning out to be measurable.</blockquote>\n<p>Automata were figures that moved by themselves thanks to hidden mechanisms: they amaze you until you see how they work. The same happens with the diagonal of a square: there is no unit, however small, that fits an exact number of times into both the diagonal and the side. This scandalised the Greeks. But the geometer who understands the proof is no longer surprised; on the contrary, he would be surprised if it were otherwise. For Aristotle wonder is the starting point, not the goal: the path ends when the cause is known.</p>\n\n<h2>Plato and Aristotle face to face</h2>\n<p>Both texts start from the same thing: recognising that one does not know. But that ignorance does not play the same role. In Socrates it is the <strong>result</strong> of a long examination of others, and the end of the road is not an answer but a conclusion about human limits: our wisdom is worth little. In Aristotle it is the <strong>starting point</strong>: wonder lasts as long as we do not know the cause and disappears when we find it. That is why Aristotle trusts that knowledge advances, whereas Socrates leaves the search always open.</p>\n<p>The price of philosophising also changes. For Socrates philosophy is a service to the city, but an annoying one: it creates enemies and ends in a death sentence. For Aristotle it is a privilege of those who already have their lives sorted out, and the most valuable thing a human being can do. And the relationship with religion changes: Socrates begins his investigation by obeying an oracle, whereas Aristotle recognises the lover of myths as a kind of philosopher, although he considers that ‘poets tell many lies’. Behind these differences lie two ideas of what philosophy is for: to disturb or to understand.</p>\n\n<h2>To think about</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>What does Socrates find in the politicians, the poets and the craftsmen? Explain each case separately without using the word ‘ignorance’.</li>\n<li>Aristotle gives two proofs that philosophy is not sought for its usefulness. Which one seems more convincing to you, and why?</li>\n<li>Find in each text the sentence where the road that begins in not knowing comes to an end. How do those two endings differ?</li>\n<li>Of the questions that Aristotle places at the origin of philosophy (the moon, the sun, the stars, the origin of everything), which are philosophical today and which have passed to science? What had to happen for that change?</li>\n<li>Think of someone who, because they are an expert in something, gives confident opinions about everything. Is the craftsmen’s ‘discord’ a defect you also see today? Give an example.</li>\n<li>Does philosophy have to be uncomfortable, as for Socrates, or is wonder enough, as for Aristotle? Defend your position and answer the best objection against it.</li>\n</ul>"
 },
 "fil-lec-pico-darwin": {
  "subject": "fil",
  "tema": "Philosophy · Topic 2 (the human being)",
  "title": "Pico and Darwin: what place does the human being occupy in nature?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 2 (the human being). Does the human being have a fixed place in nature? Two answers separated by almost four centuries: that of Pico della Mirandola and that of Charles Darwin.</p>\n<h2>Setting the scene</h2>\n<p>Pico della Mirandola (1463–1494) was a young Italian humanist of the Renaissance, with an enormous memory and ambition. At the age of twenty-three he gathered nine hundred theses drawn from Greek, Arab, Jewish and Christian philosophers, and wanted to defend them in Rome against anyone who dared to challenge them. He wrote a speech to open that debate, but Pope Innocent VIII suspended it because some of the theses seemed heretical to him, and the speech was never delivered. The title by which we know it was added later. His central idea is bold: what makes the human being worthy is not occupying a high position in the order of the world, but having no fixed position at all.</p>\n<p>Charles Darwin (1809–1882) was an English naturalist. In <em>On the Origin of Species</em> (1859) he explained the evolution of living beings by natural selection, but barely spoke of the human being. He did so twelve years later in <em>The Descent of Man</em> (1871), where he applies the same theory to our species. In the final pages he summarises his evidence and draws the conclusion that so scandalised people: the human being is one animal among others, related to the rest, and the body proves it. Pico and Darwin therefore answer the same question —what place do we occupy in nature?— and do so in almost opposite ways.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Pico: what God did not give Adam</h2>\n<blockquote>‘We have given you, Adam, no fixed place, no face of your own, no gift that is yours alone, so that the place, the face and the gifts you yourself desire you may have and possess according to your will and your own judgement. The nature of other creatures, already defined, is held within the laws I have prescribed for them. No limit confines you: you will fix your nature for yourself, by your own free choice, in whose hands I have placed you.’</blockquote>\n<p>Pico imagines creation as a distribution that has already finished when the turn of the human being arrives: every animal, every plant and every angel has its place and its way of being, and there is nothing left to give. God’s solution is to turn that lack into a privilege. The rest of the creatures have a closed nature: the lion will always be a lion and the bee will always live as a bee, because the laws of their species decide it for them. The human being, on the other hand, receives an open nature, which they will have to fix themselves. The key word is ‘choice’ (arbitrio): it does not mean whim, but the capacity to decide for oneself without a prior mould to obey. Notice that what God denies Adam (a place, a face, a gift) is precisely what allows him to choose everything.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Pico: sculptor of oneself</h2>\n<blockquote>‘We placed you in the middle of the world so that from there you could more easily look at everything in it. We made you neither heavenly nor earthly, neither mortal nor immortal, so that, almost as your own sculptor and shaper, free and honoured with that freedom, you may sculpt yourself into the form you prefer. You will be able to degenerate into the lower things, which are the beasts; you will be able, by decision of your spirit, to be reborn into the higher ones, which are divine.’</blockquote>\n<p>The image of the sculptor sums it all up. The human being does not choose among models that already exist, like someone picking clothes in a shop: they are at once the clay and the workshop, the material and the one who gives it form. That is why Pico places them ‘in the middle’: neither angel nor beast, they can look upwards and downwards. But this dignity is not a guarantee of anything. The same freedom that allows one to rise towards the divine allows one to sink into the animal: degenerating and being reborn are two equally possible paths. In Pico, up and down are moral: living only for appetites is to lower oneself to a beast; cultivating reason and spirit is to rise. Another detail matters too: this freedom is a gift, and someone gives it. Without God, the thesis changes weight, as you will see in 20th-century existentialism.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Darwin: the facts that admit no discussion</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Man resembles the lower animals closely. He resembles them in the development of the embryo, and in innumerable points of structure and constitution, both those of the highest importance and those of the least. He retains rudiments, and is liable now and then to abnormal reversions. These are facts that admit of no dispute.’</blockquote>\n<p>Darwin does not speak of God or of Adam: he speaks of embryos, bones and muscles. His starting point is observable facts. The human embryo, in its first weeks, closely resembles that of other vertebrates. Our body shares basic structures with those of other mammals, including those that serve no purpose. We keep ‘rudiments’, organs that no longer perform the function they once had, such as the coccyx, the remnant of a tail, or the muscles that move the ears in other animals and that in us barely work. And sometimes ‘reversions’ appear, traits of an ancestor that reappear in a particular individual. For Darwin, these details are not manufacturing errors but signatures of our history: if a trait makes no sense in the design, it has to make sense in the genealogy.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Darwin: a hairy ancestor</h2>\n<blockquote>‘The great principle of evolution stands up clear and firm when these facts are considered together with others: the mutual affinities of the members of the same group, where they lived and where they live, and the order in which they appear in the layers of the earth. […] We then learn that man is descended from a hairy quadruped, furnished with a tail, which probably lived in trees and inhabited the Old World. […] The main conclusion of this work is that man is descended from some lowly organised form. This will, I regret to think, be very distasteful to many.’</blockquote>\n<p>Here lies the strength of Darwin’s argument: no single piece of evidence is enough, but all of them together point in the same direction. The resemblance between species, their distribution across the continents and the order in which fossils appear in the strata of the earth are all explained at once if they descend from common ancestors. That is why he says the principle of evolution is seen ‘clear and firm’ only when the facts are looked at together. The conclusion is uncomfortable, and he knows it: the human being is not the fruit of a separate creation, but one more descendant of the tree of life. Watch the verb: in Darwin, ‘to descend’ (in English, <em>descent</em>) is genealogy, knowing who you come from, not falling or degrading. In the full text Darwin also uses words like ‘savage’ and ‘barbarians’ to speak of other peoples. They are his own and reflect a prejudice of his time that his own theory does not support: if we all come from the same ancestor, no people is closer to it than another. Knowing how to separate an excellent argument from a prejudice is part of reading philosophy.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Darwin: the indelible stamp</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Man may be excused for feeling some pride at having risen to the very summit of the organic scale, even though he has not risen by his own efforts. […] But here we are not concerned with hopes or fears, only with the truth, as far as our reason permits us to discover it. […] Man has noble qualities. He feels compassion for the most degraded. He extends his benevolence not only to other men but to the humblest living creature. His almost divine intellect has come to understand the movements and constitution of the solar system. With all these sublime powers, man still bears in his bodily frame the indelible stamp of his lowly origin.’</blockquote>\n<p>The end of the book is almost a portrait. Darwin does not deny human greatness: he recognises our compassion, our kindness even towards animals and an intelligence capable of understanding the solar system. But that greatness does not erase the origin: we carry it ‘stamped’ on our body. Notice his attitude: he is not interested in whether the conclusion comforts or frightens us, only in whether it is true according to the evidence. There is, moreover, a curious tension. Darwin speaks of the ‘summit’ of the ‘organic scale’, an old image that places living beings on a ladder with us at the top. But his theory describes rather a branching tree, where no branch is higher than another: each species is adapted to its environment. Can Darwin, with his own theory, go on speaking of up and down?</p>\n\n<h2>Pico and Darwin, face to face</h2>\n<p>Both texts answer the same question: what is the place of the human being in the order of things? Pico answers that they have none, and that therein lies their dignity: we are the only creature without a fixed nature. Darwin answers that they do have one, that of an animal among others, and that they carry it written on their body. For Pico, what defines us is what we will do; for Darwin, where we come from.</p>\n<p>They also differ in how they argue. Pico puts his thesis in the mouth of God, and that gives it an authority that does not need to be proved; in exchange, it only convinces those who already accept that religious framework. Darwin renounces any authority other than the evidence: anyone can check it, discuss it or complete it. Both use a vertical axis with an up and a down, but they do not move the same thing along it: in Pico one rises or falls through one’s own decisions, and the scale is moral; in Darwin, the species ‘has risen’ without effort of its own, through a natural history that no one directed.</p>\n<p>Do they exclude each other? Not entirely. Darwin explains where our body comes from, but does not say what we should do with it. Pico, on the other hand, does not speak of biology but of freedom. Many later thinkers have tried to keep both ideas: we are animals with an evolutionary history and, at the same time, the only ones who ask themselves what to do with their lives. Whether that is enough to speak of ‘dignity’ is for you to think about.</p>\n\n<h2>To think about</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>What exactly does God not give Adam, and why does he deny it to him? Answer using the text and without using the word ‘freedom’.</li>\n<li>Darwin says that the principle of evolution only ‘stands up clear and firm’ when the facts are considered together. What would his argument lack if he took each piece of evidence separately?</li>\n<li>Both authors speak of rising and falling. What rises or falls in Pico, and what in Darwin? Which of the two has an up and a down that his own theory does not allow him to have?</li>\n<li>Pico puts his thesis in the mouth of God; Darwin says he is concerned only with the truth and the evidence. What does each gain and lose with that strategy?</li>\n<li>Does Pico need an immaterial soul to sustain his thesis? Could someone who thinks that only matter exists accept that the human being ‘sculpts’ themselves?</li>\n<li>If Darwin is right about where we come from, does anything remain standing of what Pico says we are? Take a side and answer the best objection against your position.</li>\n</ul>"
 },
 "fil-lec-descartes-bacon": {
  "subject": "fil",
  "tema": "Philosophy · Topic 3 (knowledge and truth)",
  "title": "Descartes and Bacon: doubt and the idols",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 3 (knowledge and truth). Two ways of cleaning the mind before using it: Descartes’s doubt and Bacon’s idols.</p>\n<h2>Setting the scene</h2>\n<p>Francis Bacon (1561–1626) was an English lawyer and politician: he became Lord Chancellor and fell from grace accused of taking bribes. In 1620 he published the <em>Novum Organum</em>, ‘new instrument’, a title that is already a challenge: the old instrument of thinking was Aristotle’s logic, and Bacon wanted another, designed to investigate nature starting from cases. He writes in numbered aphorisms, short sentences that can be read on their own.</p>\n<p>René Descartes (1596–1650), a Frenchman and a mathematician as well as a philosopher, published the <em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em> in 1641, in Latin. They are not a treatise setting out results, but a first-person exercise spread over six days: the reader has to make the meditation with him. In the first two he tears down everything he believed he knew to see whether anything resists.</p>\n<p>The two share a purpose —cleaning the understanding before setting out to know— but, as you will see, they do not distrust the same thing.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Descartes: the senses have already deceived me</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Everything I have admitted until today as most true I have received from the senses or through the senses; and I have found that these sometimes deceive, and it is prudent never to trust entirely those who have deceived us even once.’</blockquote>\n<p>Descartes does not set out to prove that everything is false; he is looking for something that is impossible to doubt. His first step is easy to accept: almost everything we know reaches us through sight, hearing or touch, and we have all seen a stick that looks broken in water or a distant tower that looks round. If a source has failed us once, it cannot serve as a secure foundation. He himself notes that this argument is not enough: that I am here, sitting by the fire, with a paper in my hand, seems beyond doubt, and denying it would be madness.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Descartes: the dream argument</h2>\n<blockquote>‘How many times does nighttime rest convince me of the usual things —that I am here, dressed in a gown, sitting by the fire— when in reality I am lying between the sheets without clothes! […] And thinking about it more carefully I see so clearly that waking cannot be distinguished from sleeping by any certain sign that I am left stunned; and that very stupor almost convinces me that I am dreaming.’</blockquote>\n<p>Here the doubt takes a leap. It is no longer a matter of small errors, but of my having no sure sign to know whether I am awake. While we dream, the dream seems real to us; therefore, the feeling that ‘this is real’ proves nothing. Notice what survives: the sciences that study composite things, such as physics or medicine, are put in doubt, but arithmetic and geometry are not, because ‘whether I am asleep or awake, two and three together make five’.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Descartes: the evil genius</h2>\n<blockquote>‘I shall suppose, then, not that there is a supremely good God, the source of truth, but an evil genius, at once extremely powerful and cunning, who has employed all his energy in deceiving me. […] I shall remain stubbornly fixed in this meditation, and so, if it is not in my power to know anything true, it is at least in my power not to give my assent to what is false.’</blockquote>\n<p>To bring down mathematics too, an extreme hypothesis is needed: what if an immensely powerful being made me go wrong every time I add two and three? Descartes does not believe in that genius; he invents him on purpose as the worst imaginable enemy, to see what withstands even his attack. It is a deliberately exaggerated doubt, a stress test. And note the last sentence: even if I cannot know anything, I can still refuse to accept what is false. The will not to be deceived is already at work.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Descartes: I am, I exist</h2>\n<blockquote>‘So, without any doubt, I also exist, if he deceives me; and let him deceive me as much as he can: he will never bring it about that I am nothing so long as I think that I am something. […] Thinking? Here I find it: thought exists; this alone cannot be torn from me. I am, I exist: that is certain. But for how long? As long as I think.’</blockquote>\n<p>This is the firm point he was looking for. For the genius to deceive me, I have to be there, thinking; doubting is already thinking, and so is being mistaken. That is why the sentence ‘I am, I exist’ is true every time I think it: denying it confirms it. Then Descartes asks what this ‘I’ is, and answers that, for the moment, he can only be sure that it is ‘a thing that thinks’: a thing that doubts, understands, affirms, denies, wills, refuses, imagines and feels. He does not yet say that it has a body; that certainty will have to be earned later.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Bacon: the four idols</h2>\n<blockquote>‘There are four kinds of idols that beset human minds. […] The first kind I call idols of the tribe; the second, idols of the cave; the third, idols of the marketplace; the fourth, idols of the theatre. […] The human understanding is like an uneven mirror placed before the rays of things: it mixes its own nature with the nature of things, and thus twists and spoils it.’</blockquote>\n<p>An ‘idol’ (from the Greek <em>eidolon</em>, image) is a false image that slips into our heads and that we take for the thing itself. Bacon distinguishes four according to their origin. Those of the <strong>tribe</strong> we all have because we are human: our mind is a mirror that distorts. Those of the <strong>cave</strong> belong to each individual: character, education, books read, people we admire. Those of the <strong>marketplace</strong> (the public square) are born of language: badly used or empty words that drag us into endless arguments. Those of the <strong>theatre</strong> come from accepted philosophical systems, which for Bacon are like plays performed on a stage: worlds of fiction.</p>\n\n<h2>6. Bacon: the shipwrecked who are not in the picture</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Hence the answer was a good one of the man who, being shown in a temple the votive tablets of those who had fulfilled their vows after escaping a shipwreck, was pressed with questions about whether he now acknowledged the power of the gods; and he asked in return: “And where are those painted who drowned after making their vows?” […] And it is the proper and perpetual error of the human understanding to be moved and excited more by affirmatives than by negatives […]; and even more: in establishing any true axiom, the force of the negative instance is greater.’</blockquote>\n<p>Once something convinces us, the mind looks for what confirms it and overlooks what contradicts it. Today we call this <strong>confirmation bias</strong>. The example is brilliant because it does not dispute the belief, but the sample: in the temple only the tablets of those who were saved hang; those who prayed and drowned could not paint any. The same happens with horoscopes or omens: we remember the hits and forget the misses. Hence Bacon’s rule: a single case against weighs more than many in favour. For him an axiom is not an evident starting point, but a general conclusion obtained from cases, and one that does not fit is enough to make it totter.</p>\n\n<h2>7. Bacon: neither ant nor spider, but bee</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Empiricists, like the ant, only pile up and use what they have piled up; rationalists, like spiders, spin their web out of themselves. The bee, on the other hand, takes a middle way: it gathers its material from the flowers of the garden and the field, but transforms and digests it with a faculty of its own. […] So from a closer and more honourable alliance between these two faculties —the experimental and the rational—, which has not been made until now, there is much good to be expected.’</blockquote>\n<p>Bacon closes with the image of three animals. The ant collects data without understanding them; the spider weaves theories drawn from its own head without looking at the world. The bee does the right thing: it gathers material from outside (observations and experiments) and works it up with reason. Although Bacon is counted among the fathers of empiricism, here he does not defend experience alone, but a collaboration between experience and reason. It is almost a portrait of modern scientific work.</p>\n\n<h2>Two distrusts that do not point to the same place</h2>\n<p>Both texts begin by pulling the ground from under our feet, and that is why they seem more alike than they are. Descartes distrusts above all the senses and saves himself with an act of pure reason: there is one thought, ‘I exist’, that no deception can make false. Bacon distrusts exactly the opposite: his idols are not failures of the eyes, but deformations of the understanding itself, and they appear precisely when we reason. If Descartes is right, the solution is to think more and better; for Bacon, trusting what the mind finds evident is part of the problem, and the remedy is to go out and look at cases, especially those that go against us.</p>\n<p>There is another difference. Descartes’s doubt is not scepticism: the sceptic doubts in order to go on doubting, whereas Descartes doubts in order to stop doubting, and abandons doubt as soon as he finds his firm point. It is a doubt with an expiry date. Bacon’s idols, on the other hand, never expire: they are not cured once and for all, they are counterbalanced by method. And while Descartes meditates alone, shut in his room, Bacon thinks of collective work, like that of bees in the hive.</p>\n\n<h2>To think about</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Descartes’s doubt advances in three steps: the senses, the dream and the evil genius. What does each bring down? Which one is needed to doubt that two and three make five?</li>\n<li>Give an example of your own for each of Bacon’s four idols. Which do you share with all humanity, which because you speak a language, which because of what you have read or seen, and which is yours alone?</li>\n<li>Can someone free themselves from the idols of the marketplace alone, which arise from talking with others? Could a team carry out Descartes’s meditation, or does each person have to do it on their own?</li>\n<li>Find a present-day case similar to that of the temple tablets: an advertisement, a news item or a testimonial that only shows successes. What data are missing in order to judge it?</li>\n<li>Bacon is suspicious of our own head; Descartes trusts that something is evident to it. Can both be held at once? Take a side, defend it with a case and answer the best objection against your position.</li>\n</ul>"
 },
 "fil-lec-epicuro-meneceo": {
  "subject": "fil",
  "tema": "Philosophy · Topic 5 (ethics: the Hellenistic schools)",
  "title": "Epicurus, Letter to Menoeceus: pleasure as a life without fear",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 5 (ethics: the Hellenistic schools). Epicurus and the ethics of pleasure as serenity: how to live without fear of the gods, of death or of fate.</p>\n<h2>Setting the scene</h2>\n<p>Epicurus (341–270 BC) was born into an Athenian family settled on the island of Samos. At about the age of thirty-five he established himself in Athens, bought a house with a garden and founded there a community of friends who studied and lived together: the Garden, which also admitted women and slaves, something rare in his time. He lived in the restless world that followed Alexander the Great, when the Greek cities had lost their independence and the question was no longer how to govern the <em>polis</em>, but how to be happy oneself amid disorder. He wrote a great deal, but almost all of it was lost; what we have we owe above all to Diogenes Laertius, who centuries later copied three of his letters into his biography. The <em>Letter to Menoeceus</em> is the shortest and the clearest: a summary of his ethics addressed to a young friend. It should be read as a recipe against fear, because that is what it aims to be: philosophy, for Epicurus, is a medicine of the soul.</p>\n<h2>1. It is never too early or too late to do philosophy</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Let no one put off philosophy until later because they are young, nor grow tired of it because they are already old: when it comes to caring for the health of the soul, no one is ever too young or too old. Whoever says that the age for philosophy has not yet come for them, or that it has already passed, is like someone who says that it is not yet the moment to be happy, or that the moment is gone. […] So we must practise the things that bring happiness, since if we have it, we lack nothing, and if we do not have it, everything we do is aimed at getting it.’</blockquote>\n<p>The letter opens with a surprising idea: doing philosophy is the same as caring for the health of the soul, and so it has no age limit. Just as nobody would say ‘I am too young to be healthy’, nobody should say it is too early or too late to think about how to live. Look at the last sentence: happiness is the end of everything we do, the thing for the sake of which we do everything else. Epicurus shares this with Aristotle; what changes is what that happiness consists in. And the word ‘practise’ matters: philosophy is not a theory to learn for the exam, but daily training, like an athlete’s.</p>\n<h2>2. The gods exist, but they are not watching you</h2>\n<blockquote>‘First of all, think of god as a living being that does not perish and is blessed, just as the idea we all have of him portrays him, and do not hang on him anything that does not fit with his immortality or that is at odds with his blessedness. […] The impious person is not the one who rejects the gods the majority believe in, but the one who attributes to them what the majority think about them.’</blockquote>\n<p>Epicurus is not an atheist: he says the gods exist. But he starts from what we all understand by ‘god’, a happy being that cannot die, and draws the consequence. A perfectly happy being needs nothing: it does not get angry, does not reward or punish, does not demand sacrifices, because all of that would be a sign that it lacks something. So the gods live in peace and take no interest in us. The final blow is bold: the impious person is not the one who denies the gods of ordinary people, but the one who attributes anger and revenge to them, because that drags them down. ‘The majority’ is not an insult to the poor; it means whoever repeats what people say without having examined it. The practical result is what matters to him: if nobody is watching you from the heavens, religion stops being a source of fear.</p>\n<h2>3. Death is nothing to us</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Get used to the idea that death is no concern of ours at all, because good and evil lie in what is felt, and death consists in no longer feeling. […] So the evil that causes the most horror, death, has nothing to do with us: while we are here, death has not arrived, and when death arrives, we are no longer here.’</blockquote>\n<p>This is Epicurus’s most famous argument, and it is built as a chain of reasoning. First premise: something is only good or bad if it is felt (pleasure and pain are sensations). Second premise: dying is ceasing to feel, because for Epicurus, who is an atomist, the soul is also made of atoms and scatters along with the body. Conclusion: death cannot be an evil for the person who dies, because there is no longer anyone to suffer it. What harms us is not death, but the fear of it while we are alive, and that fear is pointless: what does no harm when it arrives only hurts, and in vain, while we wait for it. However, everything depends on the first premise. If something can be bad for you without your noticing it (for example, losing everything you would have lived), the argument starts to wobble.</p>\n<h2>4. Quality, not quantity</h2>\n<blockquote>‘The wise person neither rejects life nor is afraid of ceasing to live: life is not a burden to them, nor do they think that not living is an evil. And just as with food they do not simply choose the biggest portion but the most enjoyable one, so with time they do not seek to enjoy the longest, but the most pleasant.’</blockquote>\n<p>If death is not an evil, there is no point in being obsessed with living as many years as possible either. The comparison with food is very clear: no sensible person prefers a huge, bland plateful to a small, delicious one. The same goes for life: what counts is how it is lived, not how long it lasts. Notice also the balance in the first sentence: the wise person does not seek death, but does not live in terror either. Neither contempt for life nor a craving for immortality.</p>\n<h2>5. Knowing which desires are worth it</h2>\n<blockquote>‘We have to do the sums: among our desires, some are natural and others empty; of the natural ones, some are necessary and others merely natural; and of the necessary ones, some are necessary for happiness, others for the body to be at rest, and others for life itself.’</blockquote>\n<p>Here is the practical tool of Epicurean ethics: a classification of desires. There are <strong>natural and necessary</strong> desires (eating when you are hungry, keeping warm, having friends), which are easy to satisfy and whose absence hurts. There are <strong>natural but not necessary</strong> desires (eating an exquisite dish instead of a simple one), which can be enjoyed without depending on them. And there are <strong>empty</strong> desires (fame, luxury, power, immortality), which arise from false opinions and are never satisfied: the more you have, the more you want. The key lies in ‘doing the sums’: being happy means thinking before desiring, not following every impulse.</p>\n<h2>6. Which pleasure?</h2>\n<blockquote>‘So when we say that pleasure is the goal, we do not mean the pleasures of spendthrifts or those that consist in feasting, as some people think through ignorance, because they disagree with us or because they misunderstand us, but rather having no pain in the body and no turmoil in the soul. What makes life pleasant is not drinking bouts or one party after another […], but calm reasoning that looks into why each thing should be chosen or rejected and that drives out the opinions that most unsettle the soul.’</blockquote>\n<p>This is the passage to keep firmly in mind so as not to fall into the trap. Epicurus is a hedonist: he says that pleasure is the good and the end of life. But his pleasure is not partying or intense sensation, but a state of calm: suffering no pain in the body (<em>aponía</em>) and no disturbance in the soul (<em>ataraxia</em>). Even in his own time he was accused of being a libertine, which is why he defends himself so forcefully. Non-stop partying does not bring that calm; on the contrary, it brings hangovers, dependencies and anxiety. The happy life is built by prudence, the ‘calm reasoning’ that weighs up what is worth choosing and what is worth avoiding. That is why, although today ‘epicurean’ sometimes means ‘lover of luxury’, the Garden lived on bread, water and conversation.</p>\n<h2>7. Necessity, chance and what is up to us</h2>\n<blockquote>‘For whom would you rank above the person who thinks of the gods with piety, has no fear at all of death […] and laughs at fate, which some present as the master of all things? This person holds, instead, that some things happen by necessity, others by chance and others through us: necessity is answerable to no one, chance is plainly unstable, and what is up to us has no master, and that is why it is natural for blame and its opposite to go with it.’</blockquote>\n<p>The portrait of the wise person brings together everything above and adds one last fear to overcome: fate. The Stoics, the other great Hellenistic school, taught that everything happens through a chain of necessary causes. Epicurus rejects this and sorts what happens into three boxes: what is necessary, what happens by chance and what is up to us. This third box is freedom, and he justifies it in an interesting way: if someone deserves blame or praise for what they do, it must have been up to them. Later on, the letter even says that it prefers the myths about the gods to the fate of the natural philosophers, because a god can be prayed to, but necessity cannot. Whoever masters these fears, Epicurus concludes, will live like a god among human beings.</p>\n<h2>Epicurus and the Stoics: two paths to calm</h2>\n<p>Epicureanism and Stoicism were born almost at the same time in the same Athens and seek the same thing: a happiness that does not depend on luck or politics, based on tranquillity of soul. But they disagree on almost everything else. For Epicurus the good is pleasure understood as the absence of pain; for the Stoics the only good is virtue, and pleasure is indifferent. Epicurus believes in a world of atoms and chance where the gods do not intervene; the Stoics, in a cosmos ordered by a divine reason (the <em>logos</em>) in which everything happens by necessity, and wisdom consists in accepting it. Epicurus advises withdrawing with friends to the Garden; the Stoics, doing one’s duty in the city. One wants to free you from fate; the others, to have you embrace it.</p>\n<h2>To think about</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Reconstruct Epicurus’s argument about death in two sentences and point out the premise on which everything depends. Does it seem true to you?</li>\n<li>Make a diagram with the three types of desires and put an example from your own life in each box. Which of your vain desires cost you the most?</li>\n<li>Why, according to Epicurus, is the impious person not the one who denies the gods of the many, but the one who attributes to them the opinions of the many? Look at what he understands by ‘god’.</li>\n<li>If Epicurus lived today, what would he say about advertising and social media, which constantly manufacture desires?</li>\n<li>Compare Epicurean tranquillity with Stoic tranquillity: which advice seems more useful to you when facing a failed exam, a break-up or an illness? Defend your answer and respond to the best objection against it.</li>\n</ul>"
 },
 "fil-lec-agustin-vida-feliz": {
  "subject": "fil",
  "tema": "Philosophy · Topic 5 (ethics)",
  "title": "Saint Augustine, On the Happy Life (ch. 2, §8, 10 and 11)",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 5 (ethics). Saint Augustine: happiness does not lie in the goods of fortune, but in possessing God.</p>\n<h2>Setting the scene</h2>\n<p>Augustine wrote <em>On the Happy Life</em> in the autumn of 386, in a country house near Milan, shortly before his baptism. It is a dialogue that takes place during his birthday, in the form of a banquet: Augustine, his mother Monica and the young men Trygetius and Licentius speak. Here sections 8, 10 and 11 of chapter 2 are collected (9 is not included); the numbers in square brackets order the paragraphs of each section.</p>\n\n<h2>Section 8</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[1] —And the soul, what do you say? —I asked them—. Does it not also have its food? Do you not think that knowledge is its principal fare?</p>\n<p>—Yes, of course —said my mother—, because the soul is nourished by nothing else than the knowledge and understanding of things.</p>\n<p>[2] As Trygetius was not entirely convinced by that statement, my mother said to him:</p>\n<p>—And haven’t you yourself explained today how and from where the soul is nourished? Shortly after you sat down to eat you said that you had not noticed the cup we were using, because your head was lost in other matters; and yet neither your hands nor your mouth stopped. Where was your mind, your soul, then, while you ate without paying attention? Believe me: at that moment too the soul was feeding on its own food —its imaginings and thoughts—, eager to grasp something.</p>\n<p>[3] These words set off a discussion, and I said to them:</p>\n<p>—Will you not grant me that the souls of great scholars and students are, in their own order, much richer and broader than those of people who lack knowledge?</p>\n<p>—That is clear —they all answered at once.</p>\n<p>—With good reason, then, we can say that the souls of people without discipline or sound learning are hungry and empty.</p>\n<p>—I think —said Trygetius— that their souls are full, but of evils and wickedness.</p>\n<p>[4] —Exactly that —I said to him—. Do not doubt it: that is sterility and hunger of the soul. For just as bodies deprived of food often fall ill and fill with ulcers as a result of hunger, in the same way their souls are full of diseases, which are the sign of their fasting. Indeed, the ancients considered nequitia, or wickedness, the mother of all vices, because it is something that is nothing. And the virtue opposed to that vice is called frugality (<em>frugalitas</em>). Just as that word comes from <em>frux</em>, that is, ‘fruit’, to express a kind of spiritual fertility, the other, nequitia, comes from sterility, from nothingness, because nothingness is what flows away, dissolves, liquefies, and always dies and is lost. That is why we also call such people ‘lost’. On the other hand, what is truly something remains, stands firm, is always the same, like virtue, whose principal and noblest part is frugality and temperance. But if what I have said seems incomprehensible to you, you will surely grant me that, if the ignorant have their souls full, then for both bodies and souls there are two kinds of food: some healthy and beneficial, and others deadly and harmful.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Section 10</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[1] Then, taking up my discourse again, I continued:</p>\n<p>—Do we all want to be happy?</p>\n<p>As soon as I had finished the sentence, they all said yes with one voice.</p>\n<p>—And is a person happy when they do not have what they desire?</p>\n<p>—No —they all replied.</p>\n<p>—And will the person who has everything they want be happy?</p>\n<p>Then my mother replied:</p>\n<p>—If they want good things and get them, yes; but if they want bad things, even if they get them, they will be wretched.</p>\n<p>[2] Smiling and glad, I said to her:</p>\n<p>—Mother, you have conquered the fortress of philosophy! Cicero, in the book entitled <em>Hortensius</em>, made the defence and praise of philosophy, and you only lack his words to express it as he did. Here is what everyone says —without being philosophers— and are even ready to argue for: that those who live as they wish are happy. A grave error! For the height of wretchedness is to have an unsuitable desire. To obtain something unsuitable is far more unfortunate than not obtaining something one desires. For a twisted will brings many more misfortunes than good luck brings benefits.</p>\n<p>[3] My mother approved these words with lively exclamations, and for a moment we forgot that she was a woman, convinced that we were before a great sage. I, as far as my strength allowed, wondered from what divine source she drank such profound truths.</p>\n<p>—Tell us now —said Licentius— what the person who seeks happiness should desire and in what things they should find their satisfaction.</p>\n<p>—On your birthday you will invite me, and I will accept with great affection everything you offer me. In the same way I want you today to sit at the banquet in my house, without asking for what has not been prepared.</p>\n<p>[4] He was left ashamed and repentant, and I added:</p>\n<p>—On one thing we agree: a person cannot be happy who lacks what they want, but neither is one simply happy who has everything they want. Is that not so?</p>\n<p>They all accepted it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Section 11</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[1] —Answer me now: whoever is not happy, is that person wretched?</p>\n<p>They all agreed without hesitation.</p>\n<p>—Therefore, everyone who does not have what they want is wretched.</p>\n<p>They all accepted it.</p>\n<p>—What, then, should the human being seek in order to attain happiness? This dish too will not be lacking at our banquet to satisfy Licentius’s hunger, for, in my opinion, it must be something that it is enough to want in order to obtain it.</p>\n<p>They all saw it clearly.</p>\n<p>[2] —Therefore —I said—, it must be something permanent and secure, which does not depend on fortune nor is subject to the ups and downs of life. For what is passing and mortal cannot be had as one wishes, nor at the moment one wishes.</p>\n<p>They all accepted it, but Trygetius said:</p>\n<p>—Fortunately, there are those who possess in abundance passing and pleasant things that fortune has given them and that satisfy all their desires.</p>\n<p>—And what do you say —I asked him— of someone who fears losing something? Are they happy?</p>\n<p>—Not at all.</p>\n<p>—And can someone live without fear who can lose what they love?</p>\n<p>—No, they cannot —he replied.</p>\n<p>—The things that fortune gives can be lost; therefore, those who possess and love them cannot be happy.</p>\n<p>[3] Trygetius admitted it. Then my mother said:</p>\n<p>—Even if they were sure of not losing those goods, those goods still cannot give them true satiety; and the more needy they are, the more wretched they will be.</p>\n<p>I replied to him:</p>\n<p>—And what do you say of someone who lives surrounded by those goods, but has set a limit and measure to their desire and lives with temperance? Does that person not seem happy to you?</p>\n<p>—They will not be happy because of those goods —said my mother—, but because they use them with moderation.</p>\n<p>[4] —Very good —I said to her—; my question admitted no other answer, and you could not have answered otherwise. Let us conclude, then, that whoever wants to be happy must seek permanent goods, which no blow of fortune can snatch away.</p>\n<p>—We have long possessed that truth —said Trygetius.</p>\n<p>—Do you think that God is eternal and permanent?</p>\n<p>—That is so evident —said Licentius— that it would not even be necessary to ask.</p>\n<p>All the others also accepted it with devotion.</p>\n<p>—Therefore, whoever possesses God is happy.</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<h2>Logical analysis of some sentences</h2>\n<p>In the Logic Corner (Classic Arguments tab) there is an analysis of several sentences from this text: their logical form, their Euler diagram and their truth table.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#logica/clasicos/monica\">Monica (section 10): ‘If someone desires good things and has them, they are happy’</a>: a sufficient condition.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#logica/clasicos/agustin\">Augustine (section 11): ‘Only the person who desires what they cannot lose can attain happiness’</a>: a necessary condition, and the trap that ‘only’ is not ‘all’.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#logica/clasicos/agustin2\">Variant (sections 10 and 11): ‘…which is the end that we all necessarily seek’</a>: the literal reading and that of ‘whoever possesses God is happy’, with the circle of the unhappy.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#logica/ejercicios/contrafactico\">A classroom sentence that argues with the text: ‘…they would be happy if they could not stop desiring it’</a>: one can lose the desire, not just the object.</li>\n</ul>"
 },
 "fil-lec-aristoteles-termino-medio": {
  "subject": "fil",
  "tema": "Philosophy · Topic 5 (ethics)",
  "title": "Aristotle: virtue as the mean (Nicomachean Ethics II 6)",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 5 (ethics). Aristotle’s definition of virtue as the mean.</p>\n<h2>Setting the scene</h2>\n<p>Aristotle (384–322 BC) was a student of Plato for twenty years and then founded his own school in Athens, the Lyceum. The <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> is not a book designed to be sold: it is, very probably, the notes for his lectures, and that is why the prose is dry and does not stop to repeat anything. His underlying question is what a good life is, and his answer is that happiness (<em>eudaimonia</em>) consists in living in accordance with virtue. The passage you are going to read is in Book II and contains the most famous definition of virtue in the history of ethics, that of the mean. It should be read in full, because it is almost always quoted in part.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Two ways of being in the middle</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Of anything continuous that can be divided, one can take more, less or an equal part, and this holds whether we look at the thing itself or at ourselves. The equal is a kind of midpoint between what is too much and what is too little. By the mean of the thing I mean the one that is the same distance from one extreme as from the other: that one is single and the same for everyone. By the mean relative to us, I mean what neither goes too far nor falls short: that one is neither single nor the same for everyone. So, if ten is too much and two too little, six is taken as the mean according to the thing, because it exceeds the one and is exceeded by the other by the same amount: it is the mean according to arithmetical proportion.’</blockquote>\n<p>Aristotle begins by distinguishing two ‘means’. The first is the mathematical one: between 2 and 10 lies 6, for you, for me and for anyone. The second is the mean ‘relative to us’, the one that depends on who you are and on your situation. The Greek word we translate as ‘mean’ is <em>mesotes</em>, and it does not mean half or average, but the right position between two extremes. Be careful here, because in everyday language ‘mean’ makes us think of the arithmetical average, and that is precisely what the text is about to rule out in the very next line.</p>\n\n<h2>2. The trainer and the athlete</h2>\n<blockquote>‘The mean relative to us, on the other hand, is not worked out in this way. If ten minas of food are too much for someone and two are too little, the gym trainer will not tell them to eat six, because that too may be too much or too little for the person who is going to eat it: little for Milo, a lot for someone who has just started training. […] In this way, everyone who masters a craft avoids what is too much and what is too little, seeks the mean and chooses it; not the mean of the thing, though, but the mean relative to us.’</blockquote>\n<p>A mina was a measure of weight of a little under half a kilo, so ten minas are about four and a half kilos of food. Milo of Croton was the most famous wrestler in Greece, legendary for how much he ate. The example comes straight from the gym: a good trainer does not give everyone the same diet, but the one that suits each person. Notice what Aristotle achieves with this. On the one hand, the mean really exists and you can hit it or miss it (eating four kilos is a mistake for a beginner). On the other, it cannot be calculated with a formula that is the same for everyone. If it could, an instruction manual would be enough and there would be no need to be virtuous.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Feeling the right thing at the right time</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Every kind of expertise does its work well by keeping its eye on the mean and bringing what it produces towards it; that is why people often say of a successful piece of work that nothing can be taken away from it or added to it […]. Well then, if virtue is more precise and better than any craft, as nature is too, it must be able to aim at the mean. I am referring to ethical virtue, virtue of character, because it is concerned with feelings and actions, and in these one can go too far, fall short or hit the mean. Being afraid, feeling confident, desiring, getting angry, feeling pity and, in general, feeling pleasure or pain can happen too much or too little, and in neither case is it right. On the other hand, feeling them when one should, about the things one should, towards the people one should, for the purpose one should and in the way one should is the mean and the best, and that is exactly what belongs to virtue.’</blockquote>\n<p>The argument begins with a comparison: just as a well-made piece of work is one with nothing too much and nothing missing, a well-lived life is one that gets the measure right. But the most interesting part is the list at the end. For Aristotle, virtue is not only doing the right thing, but also feeling the right thing: getting angry is not bad in itself; what is bad is getting angry over something trivial, with someone who is not to blame, or out of all proportion. And you have to get everything right at once: the moment, the object, the person, the motive and the manner. Someone who is rightly outraged at an injustice but takes it out on their little brother has failed in one of the five things, and that is enough to miss the mean.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Missing is easy, hitting the mark is hard</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Moreover, there are many ways of going wrong (evil belongs to the unlimited, as the Pythagoreans pictured it, and good to the limited), but only one way of getting it right. That is why the one is easy and the other difficult: missing the target is easy; hitting it, difficult. For these reasons, excess and deficiency belong to vice, and the mean to virtue: <em>the noble are noble in just one way; the bad, in a thousand ways</em>.’</blockquote>\n<p>Think of a target: there is only one centre and an infinite number of points where the arrow can land outside it. The Pythagoreans arranged the world in pairs of opposites and put good on the side of what has limit and measure, and evil on the side of what does not. Aristotle takes up this idea to explain why being good is so hard: not because the good is hidden, but because there are many ways of going too far or falling short, and only one of getting it right. The sentence in italics at the end is not his own, but a line by a poet he does not name; he uses it the way someone quotes a proverb.</p>\n\n<h2>5. The definition: a mean that is an extreme</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Virtue, then, is a disposition that leads to choosing and that consists in a mean relative to us, determined by reason, in the way the prudent person would determine it. It is a mean between two vices, one of excess and the other of deficiency. […] That is why, if we look at what it is and at the definition that states its essence, virtue is a mean; but if we look at what is best and well done, it is an extreme.’</blockquote>\n<p>This is the full definition, and every piece counts. ‘Disposition’ translates <em>hexis</em>: not a passing mood, but a stable way of being, what you would do without thinking about it; that is why virtue is acquired by practice, like a habit, and not by study. ‘To choose’ translates <em>proairesis</em>, deliberate choice: someone who acts well by chance or out of fear of punishment is not yet virtuous. The mean is fixed by reason, and specifically by that of the prudent person, the one with practical wisdom (<em>phronesis</em>). And then comes the most important and most forgotten sentence: virtue is a mean in terms of what it is, but an extreme in terms of how well done it is. The brave person stands between the coward and the reckless, but is not halfway to being good: it is the best one can be in the face of danger. The mean does not mean mediocrity or half-heartedness.</p>\n\n<h2>6. What has no mean</h2>\n<blockquote>‘But not every action or every feeling admits of a mean. Some already have a name that includes wickedness, such as taking pleasure in others’ misfortune, shamelessness or envy, and, among actions, adultery, theft and murder. […] With these there is no way of ever getting it right: one always goes wrong. And here doing it well or badly does not depend on committing adultery with the right woman, at the right time and in the right way: doing any of these things is simply a mistake.’</blockquote>\n<p>This last paragraph is the most honest part of the passage. Aristotle anticipates an obvious objection: if everything is a question of measure, is there a right amount of stealing or envying? No: there are actions and feelings that are already bad by their very nature, and there is no moderate version of them. Nobody says ‘steal just the right amount, from the right person, at the right time’. In this way he saves his theory from caricature, but he lands himself in another tangle. If some things are simply wrong, whoever is looking at them, it seems that ethics also needs fixed rules, and that is exactly what the ethics of duty will hold against him: that an ethics of virtue cannot explain where those prohibitions come from.</p>\n\n<h2>Food for thought</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Using the example of the trainer, explain the difference between the ‘mean of the thing’ and the ‘mean relative to us’. Why is the first of no use to Aristotle when talking about virtue?</li>\n<li>The text asks us to feel our emotions when we should, about the things we should, towards the people we should, for the purpose we should and in the way we should. Make up an example of someone who gets everything right except one of those five things.</li>\n<li>How can virtue be both a mean and an extreme without contradicting itself? What is lost when someone understands the mean as ‘never going too far’?</li>\n<li>The definition says that the mean is fixed by ‘the prudent person’. Is that not defining virtue by the virtuous person, in other words, going round in circles? Formulate the objection and look for the best answer Aristotle could give.</li>\n<li>Find a difficult case for the list of actions with no mean: is there any situation in which lying or taking what belongs to others seems the right thing to do? Does Aristotle’s criterion hold up?</li>\n<li>Think of a real decision you have had to make. Would this ethics have helped you know what to do, or does it only describe what someone who already knows how to decide well is like?</li>\n</ul>"
 },
 "fil-lec-mill-campoamor": {
  "subject": "fil",
  "tema": "Philosophy · Topic 6 (life in society: politics)",
  "title": "Mill and Campoamor: how far does power reach and who decides?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 6 (life in society: politics). Two texts on the limits of power and on who has a voice in setting them: Mill’s harm principle and Clara Campoamor’s defence of the women’s vote.</p>\n<h2>Setting the scene</h2>\n<p><strong>John Stuart Mill</strong> (1806–1873) is the English philosopher you already know through utilitarianism: he held that some pleasures are more valuable than others. He worked throughout his adult life for the East India Company, from where British India was governed, and was a member of the House of Commons, where in 1866 he presented a petition for women to be given the vote. He wrote <em>On Liberty</em> together with Harriet Taylor, his wife, who died shortly before it was published; the book is dedicated to her. His aim is to draw a single line that says when society may interfere in someone’s life and when it may not.</p>\n<p><strong>Clara Campoamor</strong> (1888–1972) was a lawyer and one of only two women deputies in the Cortes that drafted the Constitution of the Second Republic; the other was Victoria Kent, also a lawyer. On 1 October 1931 the article granting men and women the same electoral rights was being debated. Kent had just asked for the women’s vote to be postponed: not because women were incapable, but because she feared they would vote against the Republic. Campoamor answered her with the speech from which you will read a few sentences, and the article was approved that afternoon by 161 votes to 121.</p>\n<h2>1. Mill: a single principle</h2>\n<blockquote>‘That principle is, that the sole end for which mankind are warranted, individually or collectively, in interfering with the liberty of action of any of their number, is self-protection. That the only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilised community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others. His own good, either physical or moral, is not a sufficient warrant. […] In the part which merely concerns himself, his independence is, of right, absolute. Over himself, over his own body and mind, the individual is sovereign.’</blockquote>\n<p>This is the famous <strong>harm principle</strong>. Mill states it twice in a row so that there can be no doubt: society (through laws and punishments, but also through the pressure of public opinion) can only compel you to do something in order to prevent you from harming <em>others</em>. ‘Self-protection’ means the defence of others against you, not your defence against yourself. That is why the last sentence is so strong: just as a state is sovereign within its territory, each person is sovereign over their own body and mind. Notice that Mill does not define exactly what a ‘harm’ is, and that gap is the point on which he has been most criticised.</p>\n<h2>2. Mill: advise, yes; compel, no</h2>\n<blockquote>He cannot rightfully be compelled to do or forbear because it will be better for him to do so, because it will make him happier, because, in the opinion of others, to do so would be wise, or even right. These are good reasons for remonstrating with him, or reasoning with him, or persuading him, or entreating him, but not for compelling him, or visiting him with any evil in case he do otherwise.</blockquote>\n<p>Here Mill attacks <strong>paternalism</strong>: treating adults like children who must be protected from their own decisions. If a friend of yours eats terribly, you can tell them off, give them arguments or beg them to change; what you cannot do is force them. Mill does not say that nothing matters or that we should not have opinions about other people’s lives. He says that there is a border between persuading and forcing, and that when a person’s conduct affects only themselves, the only legitimate route is the former.</p>\n<h2>3. Mill: where exactly the line lies</h2>\n<blockquote>But there is a sphere of action in which society, as distinguished from the individual, has, if any, only an indirect interest; comprehending all that portion of a person’s life and conduct which affects only himself, or if it also affects others, only with their free, voluntary, and undeceived consent and participation. When I say only himself, I mean directly, and in the first instance: for whatever affects himself, may affect others through himself […].</blockquote>\n<p>Mill marks out the ‘proper region of human liberty’: what affects only you and what you do with others who agree to it, provided that nobody is forced or deceived. But read the last sentence slowly, because Mill corrects himself: almost everything I do ends up affecting someone indirectly. If I smoke, my family suffers; if I do not study, society may lose a good doctor. If those indirect effects count, almost nothing would be exclusively my own business and the principle would protect very little; if they do not count, we need to explain why. That seam is the weak point of the criterion, and his first readers already pointed it out.</p>\n<h2>4. Mill: the only freedom worthy of the name</h2>\n<blockquote>The only freedom which deserves the name, is that of pursuing our own good in our own way, so long as we do not attempt to deprive others of theirs, or impede their efforts to obtain it. Each is the proper guardian of his own health, whether bodily, or mental and spiritual. Mankind are greater gainers by suffering each other to live as seems good to themselves, than by compelling each to live as seems good to the rest.</blockquote>\n<p>Before this paragraph Mill lists three liberties that make up that sphere: <strong>liberty of conscience</strong> (thinking, feeling and holding opinions on any subject, and also expressing them), <strong>liberty of tastes and pursuits</strong> (making the plan of your life even if others think it foolish) and <strong>liberty of association</strong> (joining with others for any purpose that harms nobody). Here he sums it up and gives his underlying reason, which is utilitarian: humanity is better off letting each person try out their own way of living than imposing the majority’s way on everyone. This is the idea of <strong>negative liberty</strong>: a space that nobody can enter.</p>\n<h2>5. Campoamor: a right in quarantine</h2>\n<blockquote>‘And why not men? Why should men, at the dawn of the Republic, have their rights, while a quarantine station is set up against those of women?’</blockquote>\n<p>A <strong>lazaretto</strong> was the building where people arriving from abroad were kept in quarantine, in case they carried some disease. This is how Campoamor answers those who said that women first had to ‘prove’ their loyalty to the Republic over a few years: nobody asked men for that test; their rights arrived on the very same day as the new regime. The argument from timing, Kent’s argument, is exposed in a single word: if the vote is a right, postponing it for some and not for others is treating it as a prize. Earlier, Campoamor had pointed out that women pay the same taxes and suffer the same laws as men.</p>\n<h2>6. Campoamor: legal is not the same as legitimate</h2>\n<blockquote>‘Deny it if you wish; you are free to do so, but only by virtue of a right that you have […] usurped, because you gave yourselves the laws; not because you have a natural right to set women aside.’</blockquote>\n<p>To <strong>usurp</strong> means to hold something that does not belong to you, and Campoamor apologises before using the word because she knows how serious it is. She does not accuse the deputies of misusing a right, but of not having it: they can deny women the vote because they themselves made the laws, that is, it is <em>legal</em>, but that does not make it <em>legitimate</em>. It is the distinction between legality and legitimacy applied to the Chamber itself. A parliament made up only of men decides which rights women have, and Campoamor asks by what title it does so.</p>\n<h2>7. Campoamor: citizen before woman</h2>\n<blockquote>‘I, Deputies, feel myself a citizen before a woman, and I consider that it would be a profound political error to leave women outside that right […].’</blockquote>\n<p>Campoamor first presents herself as a <strong>citizen</strong>: the vote is not demanded because one is a woman, but because it belongs to every member of the political community. At the end of the speech she quotes Humboldt to answer those who said that women were not yet ‘mature’: the only way to mature for freedom is to exercise it. You do not learn to vote by waiting, just as you do not learn to swim out of the water. The outcome was bitter: in 1933, the first elections in which Spanish women voted, the right won, Campoamor lost her seat and years later she died in exile.</p>\n<h2>Mill and Campoamor, face to face</h2>\n<p>The two texts are separated by seventy-two years and speak of different things, but they touch at several points. Mill was in favour of women’s suffrage and defended it in Parliament, and both rely on the same author: Mill opened his book with a quotation from Wilhelm von Humboldt on human development, and Campoamor closed her speech with another. Both think that freedom is not granted as a prize to those who have already shown they deserve it, but is learned by using it.</p>\n<p>The difference lies in the question each asks. Mill answers <em>how far</em> power may go: he draws a space that nobody can invade. But his principle does not say <em>who</em> decides what counts as harm or who writes the laws. That is precisely Campoamor’s question: legislation made for both sexes but ‘directed and shaded’ by one alone. A perfect criterion, applied by only one half, is still that half’s criterion. Mill protects negative liberty (let nobody interfere with me); Campoamor demands <strong>positive liberty</strong> (having a voice in common decisions). And the debate of 1931 was not ‘men against women’: the two women deputies stood on opposite sides. The question was whether a right is recognised because it is a right or when it is convenient.</p>\n<h2>To think about</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Mill states his principle twice in a row in different words. What does the second formulation add to the first?</li>\n<li>Does the harm principle allow a law requiring people to wear a helmet on a motorbike? Answer, and then answer again supposing that the injured are treated by a health service paid for by everyone. Does your answer change? Why?</li>\n<li>Think of something you do that you believe ‘affects only you’. Does it affect others indirectly? Should that count towards limiting it?</li>\n<li>Victoria Kent did not deny women’s right: she asked for it to wait for a better moment. Can a right be postponed for reasons of political convenience? Find a current case in which something similar is being debated.</li>\n<li>Explain with an example of your own the difference between a law that is legal but illegitimate and one that is legal and legitimate, taking the word ‘usurped’ as your starting point.</li>\n<li>Is Mill’s principle enough for Campoamor to answer Kent, or does she need something that Mill does not give her? Take a side and also write the best objection against your position.</li>\n</ul>"
 },
 "fil-lec-aristoteles-wilde": {
  "subject": "fil",
  "tema": "Philosophy · Topic 7 (aesthetics: what is art?)",
  "title": "Aristotle and Wilde: what is art for?",
  "html": "<p><strong>Topic</strong>: 7 (aesthetics: what is art?). Two opposite answers to the question of what art is for: to learn, according to Aristotle; for nothing, according to Wilde.</p>\n<h2>To get your bearings</h2>\n<p>Aristotle (384–322 BC), a pupil of Plato and the teacher of Alexander the Great, devoted a short treatise to poetry, the <em>Poetics</em>. It is not a polished book: it is rather notes for his lectures, and incomplete ones at that, because the part on comedy has been lost. In chapter 4 he does not ask what beauty is, but something more concrete: why does poetry exist? And his answer looks towards us: poetry arises from the way human beings are.</p>\n<p>Oscar Wilde (1854–1900), an Irish writer and one of the great figures of aestheticism, published <em>The Picture of Dorian Gray</em> in a magazine in 1890, the story of a young man who keeps his beauty while his portrait ages and becomes corrupt in his place. A good part of the British press accused the novel of being immoral. When he published it as a book the following year, Wilde added a preface made up of short, forceful sentences. It is not a treatise on aesthetics, but a reply to those who reproached him for having written a harmful book.</p>\n<p>A note on vocabulary: the Greek verb that many translations render as ‘imitate’ (<em>mimeisthai</em>) appears here as ‘represent’. ‘Imitate’ suggests copying what already exists; ‘represent’ comes closer to what Aristotle means: making a figure or an action present, as an actor or a playing child does.</p>\n<h2>1. Aristotle: the animal that represents</h2>\n<blockquote>‘It seems that poetry was engendered, in general, by two causes, both of them natural. For representing is natural to human beings from childhood—and in this they differ from the other animals: in that man is the most inclined to represent, and in that he makes his first lessons by representing—and it is also natural for everyone to enjoy representations.’</blockquote>\n<p>Aristotle gives two causes, and both lie in our nature, not in a decision or a fashion. The first: we represent from an early age, and that is how we learn (to speak, to behave, to play at being a father or a teacher). This, he says, separates us from the other animals: it is not that they never imitate, but that we are the ones who do it most and who learn this way. The second: we like watching representations. Without this second cause poetry would have no audience; there would be actors, but nobody would want to watch them.</p>\n<h2>2. The pleasure of looking at what we would not want to see</h2>\n<blockquote>‘A proof of this is what happens with works: things that in themselves are painful for us to look at, we look at gladly when we see them in images made with the greatest exactness; for example, the forms of the most repulsive animals, and corpses. The reason is that learning is most pleasant not only to philosophers, but equally to everyone else, even if they share in it only to a small degree.’</blockquote>\n<p>Here is the difficult case, the one that tests the theory. Nobody enjoys looking at a real corpse and yet we stand looking at a painting of a battle, the sculpture of a dead body or a well-made horror film. If the pleasure came from what is represented, that pleasure would be impossible. Aristotle explains it another way: what pleases is learning, and learning pleases everyone, not only philosophers. The exactness of the work matters because it is what allows us to recognise and understand.</p>\n<h2>3. ‘This one here is that one there’</h2>\n<blockquote>‘For they enjoy seeing images for this reason: because, as they contemplate them, they learn and infer what each thing is—that this one here is that one there. For if one happens not to have seen the original before, the work will give pleasure not as a representation, but because of its execution, or its colour, or some other such cause.’</blockquote>\n<p>Looking at an image is, for Aristotle, solving a small problem: relating what we see to something we already know (‘that is Napoleon’, ‘that is a crocodile’). In that recognition lies the pleasure proper to representation. That is why he proposes a case that works as a test: if you have never seen the original, you can still enjoy the work, but the pleasure will come from elsewhere, from the colour or from how well it is made. That is, he separates two pleasures: that of understanding and that of craftsmanship.</p>\n<h2>4. Wilde: neither moral nor immoral</h2>\n<blockquote>There is no such thing as a moral or an immoral book. Books are well written, or badly written. That is all. […] The moral life of man forms part of the subject-matter of the artist, but the morality of art consists in the perfect use of an imperfect medium.</blockquote>\n<p>Wilde answers his critics by changing the question. He does not say that art cannot deal with morality: good and evil, guilt or cruelty can be its subject. What he denies is that a work should be judged by the yardstick of morality. If art has a ‘morality’ of its own, it is that of the craft: making good use of a medium that is never perfect (words, paint, the actor’s body). A book about a murderer is not a bad book for that reason; it is bad if it is badly written.</p>\n<h2>5. The artist proves nothing</h2>\n<blockquote>No artist desires to prove anything. Even things that are true can be proved. No artist has ethical sympathies. An ethical sympathy in an artist is an unpardonable mannerism of style. No artist is ever morbid. The artist can express everything.</blockquote>\n<p>The second sentence looks like a misprint, as if a ‘not’ were missing, but it says exactly what it means, and with disdain: proving is something so ordinary that even what is true can be proved; it is not the task of art. Nor is it art’s task to take a moral side within the work and tell the reader whom to applaud. What is provocative is that Wilde does not call that a moral failing, but a defect of style, a mannerism that spoils the work. And he ends with a freedom without limits: the artist can express everything.</p>\n<h2>6. Caliban’s mirror</h2>\n<blockquote>The nineteenth century dislike of Realism is the rage of Caliban seeing his own face in a glass. The nineteenth century dislike of Romanticism is the rage of Caliban not seeing his own face in a glass. […] It is the spectator, and not life, that art really mirrors.</blockquote>\n<p>Caliban is the monstrous character in Shakespeare’s <em>The Tempest</em>. Wilde uses him to mock the public of his century: if the novel shows it what it is like (realism), it gets angry; if it does not show it (romanticism), it is angry too. The last sentence turns the argument against the critics: what each person sees in a work says more about the viewer than about the work. Whoever finds poison in a book may be looking at themselves.</p>\n<h2>7. All art is quite useless</h2>\n<blockquote>We can forgive a man for making a useful thing as long as he does not admire it. The only excuse for making a useless thing is that one admires it intensely. All art is quite useless.</blockquote>\n<p>The final sentence, the most famous, is not an insult to art but a compliment. ‘Useless’ here means that art serves no purpose other than itself: it is not a tool for teaching, convincing or improving anyone. A chair is made to sit on; a poem is not made ‘for’ anything. That is why it is justified only if it is intensely admired. The idea recalls Kant, for whom the beautiful has purposiveness without purpose, although Wilde puts it in order to shock.</p>\n<h2>Aristotle versus Wilde</h2>\n<p>The two texts answer the same question without having read each other: what is art for? Aristotle replies that it is for learning, and that the pleasure it gives us arises from that. Wilde replies that it is for nothing, and that therein lies its dignity: as soon as it is required to teach or to prove, it stops being art and becomes propaganda or a sermon.</p>\n<p>But the opposition has nuances. Wilde does not deny that art gives pleasure; he denies that this pleasure consists in learning. And there is a point on which they agree more than it seems: both hold that anything, however ugly or repulsive, can be represented in a work. Aristotle justifies it because even a painted corpse teaches us something; Wilde, because vice and virtue are merely materials for the artist. The way of writing also differs: Aristotle argues, with causes and proofs; Wilde pronounces, with aphorisms that must be accepted or rejected one by one.</p>\n<p>This discussion connects with an older formula: the Latin poet Horace asked poetry to be useful and pleasing at the same time. Aristotle would be close to that position; Wilde rejects it outright.</p>\n<h2>To think about</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Aristotle gives two natural causes for the origin of poetry. What are they? What would remain unexplained if he gave only the first?</li>\n<li>Why is the case of the image whose original you have never seen important for his argument? Think of a painting or a photo that you like without knowing what it represents: where does your pleasure come from?</li>\n<li>According to Wilde, what does the ‘morality of art’ consist of? Answer with the sentence from the text and explain it in your own words.</li>\n<li>Think of a film, a series, a song or a video game that has been accused of causing harm. What would Aristotle say? And Wilde? Who resolves the case better?</li>\n<li>‘All art is quite useless.’ Is it a provocation or a serious thesis? If you accept it, why should art be paid for with public money? If you reject it, what exactly is it for?</li>\n</ul>"
 }
};
